The  driver provides support for all devices of the environmental
services class that are attached to the  system  through  a  sup‐
ported Host Adapter, as well as emulated support for SAF‐TE (SCSI
Accessible  Fault  Tolerant  Enclosures).  The environmental ser‐
vices class generally are enclosure devices that provide environ‐
mental information such as number of power supplies (and  state),
temperature,  device  slots, and so on.  A Host adapter must also
be separately configured into the system before  a  Environmental
Services  device can be configured.  The following calls apply to
devices.  They are defined in the header file  (q.v.).   Used  to
find  out  how  many objects are driven by this particular device
instance.  Read, from the kernel, an array of SES  objects  which
contains the object identifier, which sub‐enclosure it is in, and
the  type  of the object.  Get the overall enclosure status.  Set
the overall enclosure status.  Get the status of a particular ob‐
ject.  Set the status of a particular object.  Get the associated
help text for an object (not  yet  implemented).   devices  often
have  descriptive  text  for  an object which can tell you things
like location (e.g, "left power supply").  Initialize the  enclo‐
sure.   The  device.  When the kernel is configured with enabled,
the first open to an SES device will spit out  overall  enclosure
parameters  to  the console.  The driver was written for the sub‐
system by Matthew Jacob.  This is the functional equivalent of  a
similar driver available in Solaris, Release 7.
